TY  - JOUR
TI  - Shaping the future of facility management. Market and literature insights on digital twin adoption
AU  - Martina Signorini
AU  - Alice Paola Pomè
PY  - 2025
JO  - Facilities
VL  - 43
IS  - 11月12日
SP  - 818
EP  - 834
DO  - 10.1108/f-02-2025-0029
AB  - <jats:sec>;                   <jats:title>Purpose</jats:title>;                   <jats:p>This study aims to investigate the readiness of facility management (FM) to digitalize the construction sector by adopting digital technologies, especially the digital twin (DT). After the analysis of the literature, the study investigates opportunities and challenges recognized by the market.</jats:p>;                </jats:sec>;                <jats:sec>;                   <jats:title>Design/methodology/approach</jats:title>;                   <jats:p>To collect market perceptions of digital uptake in FM, the participatory action research (PAR) methodology is adopted as a collaborative research approach that involves researchers and participants working together to identify a problem, collect and analyze data and take action to create meaningful change. PAR is iteratively applied in three phases. First, a comparison approach analyses three literature and two case studies. Second, a questionnaire survey retrieves insights from the market. Finally, interviews to FM operators are conducted to discuss the outcomes of the previous two phases.</jats:p>;                </jats:sec>;                <jats:sec>;                   <jats:title>Findings</jats:title>;                   <jats:p>Survey results and case studies comparison highlight growing interest in DT but a gap in its implementation. DT has the potential to transform FM by enhancing efficiency, predictive maintenance, energy optimization and lifecycle management. However, adoption is hindered by high costs, integration challenges and the need for specialized training. Case studies emphasize the importance of data-driven decision-making and clear client objectives. While Internet of Things, artificial intelligence and Big Data are key enablers, issues like data processing and interoperability remain. Overcoming these barriers will require standardization, cost reduction and workforce upskilling, making strategic investment in digital transformation crucial for long-term sustainability.</jats:p>;                </jats:sec>;                <jats:sec>;                   <jats:title>Originality/value</jats:title>;                   <jats:p>This paper elaborates a comprehensive assessment of FM readiness for digital transformation through the adoption of DT. By combining literature analysis, case studies comparison, surveys and interviews, the study provides a relevant perspective on the opportunities and challenges associated with DT implementation, bridging the gap between academic research and market operations.</jats:p>;                </jats:sec>
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1108/f-02-2025-0029
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Special theme: Advanced through‐life management of existing concrete structures
AU  - Sylvia Keßler
AU  - Maria Pina Limongelli
AU  - Eftychia Apostolidi
AU  - Alfred Strauss
PY  - 2025
JO  - Structural Concrete
VL  - 26
IS  - 5
SP  - 5286
EP  - 5287
DO  - 10.1002/suco.202400887
AB  - The fib is dedicated to advancing the scientific study and application of concrete construction on an international scale, aiming to improve its technical, economic, aesthetic, and environmental performance. Within its 10 commissions, Commission 3 (COM3) focuses on creating reliable procedures to ensure the safety of existing structures and determining necessary interventions to extend their operational lifespan. To support the ongoing management of these structures, Task Group (TG) 3.3 concentrates on testing and structural health monitoring. Its goals include (i) developing service life extension concepts using NDT and SHM, (ii) promoting specific testing and monitoring techniques, including those with autonomous robotic systems, (iii) assessing the reliability of NDT/SHM methods, and (iv) supporting in decision-making to transition from periodic to predictive/preventive maintenance. Extending the service life of our infrastructure is crucial for achieving sustainability goals in the construction sector. Beyond sustainability, structural safety and durability are essential for the effective through-life management of existing concrete structures. The cornerstone of any condition assessment and performance prediction is data, obtained from Non-Destructive Testing (NDT) and Structural Health Monitoring (SHM), in addition to highly developed decision-making tools. NDT and SHM techniques span a wide spectrum, from well-established methods to cutting-edge technologies such as Autonomous Robotic Systems (ARS), Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAV), image-based monitoring, and self-monitoring materials. These advanced technologies are becoming increasingly significant, generating substantial volumes of data. To manage and analyze this data efficiently, advanced tools like machine learning techniques, Building Information Modeling (BIM), Digital Shadows (DS), and Digital Twins (DT) are indispensable. These tools facilitate rapid decision-making by processing large datasets and integrating them into comprehensive models of the structural behavior. Effective decision-making in the maintenance and management of concrete structures relies on defining and estimating Key Performance Indicators (KPIs). These indicators help schedule repair and replacement measures, while considering constraints such as owner requirements, accessibility, load changes, sustainability considerations, and performance predictions. The reliability of condition assessments and performance predictions is contingent on the capabilities of the applied NDT/SHM methods. Various indicators, each with different levels of maturity, such as Value of Information (VoI), Probability of Detection (POD), Probability of Identification (POI), and Probability of Localization (POL), are used to describe the effectiveness of these methods. Currently, through-life management of concrete structures lacks standardized procedures, with many aspects still evolving within a dynamic research environment. Nevertheless, pioneering applications in practice are emerging. Innovative methods for conducting condition surveys on reinforced concrete structures can provide accurate, efficient, and noninvasive means of evaluating structural integrity and detecting defects. Advanced NDT/SHM data processing and evaluation procedures utilizing machine learning algorithms improve analysis and interpretation. By employing advanced data processing techniques, stakeholders can derive actionable insights and make informed decisions about maintenance and repairs. Reliability assessment and capability demonstrations of NDT and SHM methods assist in highlighting the potential and limitations of these techniques. Researchers can ensure their appropriate application and enhance their adoption in real-world scenarios. Data management for reinforced concrete structures using BIM, Digital Shadows, and DTs as decision support tools, emphasizes the importance of sophisticated data management systems. These tools facilitate the effective organization, visualization, and analysis of NDT/SHM data, supporting efficient decision-making processes. Decision-making frameworks should prioritize long-term performance and the extension of the service life in terms of sustainability. By anticipating potential issues and planning accordingly, stakeholders can ensure the continued functionality and safety of concrete structures. This Special Issue aims to foster a deeper understanding of the interdisciplinary approaches required for the sustainable maintenance and management for the long-term performance of reinforced concrete structures from manufacturing to end of life. Furthermore, it focus on a variety of critical topics, aiming to provide a comprehensive understanding of the latest advancements and methodologies in the field of structural concrete management. These advancements support strategies to prolong the lifespan of concrete structures. By leveraging NDT and SHM technologies, researchers and practitioners can identify potential issues early and implement timely interventions, thereby extending the service life and enhancing the durability and safety of these structures and prolonging the lifespan of concrete structures. Data sharing not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analysed during the current study. Sylvia Keßler, Engineering Materials and Building Preservation, Helmut-Schmidt-University/University of the Federal Armed Forces Hamburg, Hamburg, Germany. Email: [email protected]. Maria Pina Limongelli, Structural Engineering, Politecnico di Milano, Milan, Italy. Email: [email protected]. Eftychia Apostolidi, Technische Universität Darmstadt, Institut für Massivbau, Darmstadt, Germany. Email: [email protected]. Alfred Strauss, Department of Civil Engineering and Natural Hazards Institute of Structural Engineering, BOKU University, Vienna, Austria. Email: [email protected].
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1002/suco.202400887
